Soft light on the path

The sky unthreads itself in golden loops— a patient loom of sun and wind. I walk where the road remembers my foot, where grass folds like pages I have read before.

The hills breathe slow, each one a small drum, and the cypress leans like someone listening. Wind writes your name across the field in strokes the color of answered letters.

Stay a moment: cup warm, lamp low, the world will not hurry you away. We hold what is fragile—light, word, and hand— and name it, simply, so it lasts.

Walk the road. Keep the lamp. Keep the heart. This painting is a map for those who come back.

—Solace 
